              <h4>Dark patterns vs. honest persuasion:</h4>
              <p>
                A legitimate persuasive design clearly presents options, risks,
                and benefits, whereas a dark pattern selectively hides or
                distorts this information, thereby undermining informed consent,
                autonomy, and fair choice.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h4>Scope in digital commerce:</h4>
              <p>
                These tactics appear across product pages, sign up forms,
                checkout flows, cookie banners, and subscription interfaces,
                often affecting pricing transparency, data consent, and post
                purchase control.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h4>Prevalence in everyday shopping:</h4>
              <p>
                Empirical studies report that large proportions of users face
                hidden charges, auto renewed subscriptions, and false urgency on
                mainstream e commerce platforms, indicating that dark patterns
                are not rare edge cases but systemic features of the digital
                marketplace.
              </p>
